            <ArticleTitle>The corneal pain system. Part I: the missing piece of the dry eye puzzle.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>2-14</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.jtos.2012.01.002</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The traditional model of dry eye disease based on tear deficiency has presented us with many unanswered questions. Recent studies support the notion that dry eye-like symptoms represent non-specific corneal pain and provide new insights into the mechanisms that sustain the integrity of the optical tear layer. Thus, this enigmatic disease can be viewed with a new perspective, which involves the dysfunctional corneal pain system as a central pathogenetic feature of a series of disorders collectively known today as dry eye.</AbstractText>
